Network leg of the bus. RELOCATED 2026-08-20 from FUN_14073d730, which is NOT this function at all: that address is a movement/physics tick (a loop calling FUN_14073d780, whose body carries the "Dynamic push target" string and integrates velocity). It resolved, verified and sat at a .pdata entry the whole time - a false ok of exactly the class scripts/verify_symbol_resolve.py cannot catch, and it carried a cabi, so `R.engine.call("NamedEvent_NetSend", a, b)` would have run a physics tick over two mod-supplied pointers. Found by taking the note at its word and asking who CALLS NamedEvent_NetSendToPeer; FUN_140721630 sits immediately before it and matches the description instruction for instruction:
  if (netcomp+0x148 == 0) {
    if (netcomp+0x130 == 0) {            // authority: 0 = this machine owns it
      p = vcall(netcomp+0xc0, +0x88);    // -> ptr to the LOCAL session id (int64)
      ev+0x38 = *p;                      // stamp the event with the sender session
      repl = netcomp+0xb8;               // vslot +0x20 = net id (see Entity_GetNetId)
      if (repl) NetSendToPeer(netcomp, ev, vcall(repl, +0x18));
    } else FUN_1406e3fd0(netcomp+0x8);   // remotely owned: never emit
  }
This independently CONFIRMS netcomp+0x130 as the local/remote authority flag (0 = local), which until now rested on the two hit paths alone. Net component field set, anchored on the 344 direct callers of Entity_GetNetComponent: +0x80 ptr, +0x88 u32, +0xb8 replication object, +0xc0/+0xc8 session objects, +0x140 sub-struct, +0x149 / +0x24d / +0x24e byte flags. ev+0x38 carrying the ORIGINATING session id is the one engine-provided per-player key reaching gameplay - it is the basis for any sound entity->player join, unlike searching memory for a name string. [2026-08-20 STRUCTURE, from a full decompile] The net component C exposes identity through THREE fields, all reached by vcall: [C+0xc0]->vft[0x88](&out) returns a ptr whose [0] is the LOCAL session, and that qword is what lands in ev+0x38; [C+0xb8] is the net object (Entity_GetNetId is [C+0xb8]->vft[0x20]()), and its vft[0x18](&localSession) returns the session THIS entity replicates to -- the owner key a row->player join needs; [C+0xc8] is the scene, whose vft[0xc0](scene, session, netid, msg, 1, out) unicasts. ⚠ A session is compared as a SINGLE QWORD (`local_res20 != *param_3` in NamedEvent_NetSendToPeer), so the netcode's session id is 8 bytes while the lobby member's is a 32-character string -- they are not the same value, and no scan of the component for that string can ever hit. C+0x148 = send-disabled flag, C+0x130 = remotely-owned flag (0 = this machine owns it). [2026-08-24, Ghidra] EMITTER FIELD MAP, read off FUN_1407276a0 as it builds the event: ev+0x38 = -1 (the sender session id, stamped later by the netcode); ev+0x40 = *(float*)(*(attacker+0x30)+0x24) * DAT_140fcbee8; ev+0x48 = FUN_1407273c0(attacker) = netcomp -> *(+0xb8) -> vft[0x20], a net-object id; ev+0x50 = the embedded oCEntityHitData, copied field-for-field from the oCDtProcessedDamage (so ev+0x60 = pd+0x10 = the hit-VALUE object, damage f32 at its +0x8, and ev+0xf0 = pd+0xa0 = the source/hit-def block with the u16 attack type at +0xc8). ⚠ *(entity+0x30) is the SCENE CONTEXT -- HeroStats_OnDamageDealt applies oCTKindOfTypeTester<oCGameEventSceneContext> to that same pointer and feeds +0x24 into its recent-hits window against a SECONDS threshold. So on the sender ev+0x40 is a clock, and the payload schema publishing it as `value` would have the damage meter credit a timestamp as damage. What a RECEIVER sees there after deserialization is unmeasured -- no shipped log has ever received one of these -- so R.damage measures it instead of assuming: a strictly rising sequence is a clock, and crediting stops (F._dmg_net_value_is_clock). Settle it with one co-op session as a CLIENT, then fix the schema.
